# Copy this file to `.env` at the workspace root and fill in the value.
# `.env` is git-ignored; never commit real environment values.
#
# EPM_API_BASE_URL
#   Base URL of the EPM API, read at build time by
#   scripts/generate-environment.mjs and provided to every application through the
#   API_BASE_URL injection token from the `core` library.
#
#   Two values are legal, and which one you want depends on where the bundle runs.
#
#   `/` — LOCALLY, AND THIS IS WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. It means "the origin this app
#   is served from", and it is written through as the empty string. Under
#   `ng serve`, proxy.conf.json forwards /api to the backend on localhost:8080, so
#   the browser makes a same-origin request: no preflight, and so no CORS
#   configuration to ask the backend for (T-92). Start the backend, run
#   `npm start`, and that is the whole setup.
#
#   An absolute http:/https: URL including protocol and host — FOR ANYTHING
#   DEPLOYED. There is no proxy in a built bundle, and a deployed app is generally
#   not served from the API's origin: the patient app ships as a Capacitor bundle
#   on `capacitor://`, where a same-origin request resolves to the bundle and
#   reaches no backend at all. A trailing slash is removed during generation.
#
#   Anything else is rejected — another relative path, a host with no protocol, a
#   protocol that is not http(s). Note that the EMPTY value is rejected too, even
#   though it is what `/` resolves to: an unset variable and a blanked line both
#   look like this, and neither of them is a decision.
#
#   `npm run build`, `npm start` and `npm test` all fail before Angular starts when
#   this variable is missing or invalid.

#
# EPM_AUTH_ISSUER_BASE_URL
#   Base URL of the identity provider - Keycloak's own origin, and nothing after it.
#   Read at build time by scripts/generate-environment.mjs and turned into each
#   console's OIDC issuer.
#
#   THE REALM IS NOT PART OF THIS VALUE. Each console appends its own, because the
#   realm is which console you are rather than which environment you are in:
#   `epm-platform` for the platform console, `epm-staff` for the staff console. The
#   two halves together are the issuer, and the issuer must match that realm's
#   `.well-known/openid-configuration` document CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER
#   (LLD-INFRASTRUCTURE.md #I5). A trailing slash, or `127.0.0.1` where the realm
#   says `localhost`, produces tokens that verify and are refused anyway - which
#   looks, from the console, like sign-in working and the API rejecting everything.
#
#   Unlike EPM_API_BASE_URL there is NO `/` here. The API can be same-origin because
#   `ng serve` proxies /api to it; the identity provider never is - it is its own
#   server on its own port in every environment - so a relative value could only
#   ever reach the console itself and fetch its own index.html.
#
#   Anything that is not an absolute http(s) URL stops the build, as does leaving it
#   unset. There is no default: a console that guessed its provider would redirect
#   somebody's credentials somewhere nobody chose.

#
# EPM_ENVIRONMENT
#   Which environment this bundle is being built for: `local`, `development`,
#   `staging` or `production`.
#
#   The platform console shows it in the header, always, production included. A
#   platform administrator has the widest reach in the system, and knowing at a
#   glance which environment they are pointed at is the cheapest safeguard there
#   is against creating a real practice by accident.
#
#   Unlike EPM_API_BASE_URL, this is NOT validated at build time. Anything else -
#   a typo, or leaving it unset - reaches the console as an unrecognised value and
#   renders as `Unknown` with production's treatment, on the principle that an
#   unresolvable state is treated as the most dangerous one. Set it deliberately;
#   a build that shows `Unknown` is telling you this line is wrong.

# Same origin, through the `ng serve` proxy. Replace with an absolute URL only if
# you are pointing a local build at a deployed API - and expect CORS to be your
# problem again if you do.
EPM_API_BASE_URL=/
EPM_ENVIRONMENT=local

# Local Keycloak, as docker compose brings it up. The consoles append their realms:
# .../realms/epm-platform and .../realms/epm-staff.
EPM_AUTH_ISSUER_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8180
